The table gives a C=C bond energy of 611 kJ/mol. Using Avogadro’s constant, \(N_A=6.022\times10^{23}\ \text{mol}^{-1}\):

1. Energy per bond:
\[
E=\frac{611{,}000\ \text{J/mol}}{6.022\times10^{23}\ \text{mol}^{-1}}
=1.015\times10^{-18}\ \text{J}
\approx \boxed{6.33\ \text{eV}}
\]

2. The longest-wavelength photon with this energy has:
\[
\lambda=\frac{hc}{E}
=\frac{(6.626\times10^{-34})(3.0\times10^8)}{1.015\times10^{-18}}
\approx \boxed{196\ \text{nm}}
\]

Photons at longer wavelengths have less energy than this bond-energy threshold.
